course: chemistry
lesson: 17
title: Indicators: the red-cabbage rainbow
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Chop red cabbage, soak it in hot water, and you get a purple juice. Add a splash of vinegar and it turns pink; add a pinch of baking soda and it turns blue-green. The juice is an indicator: a colour that tells you whether something is an acid or an alkali.
explain: Inside the cabbage is a colour substance whose tiny particles change shape in acid or alkali, and a different shape means a different colour. Blueberries hold the same kind of colour, which is why blueberry pancakes with too much baking soda get green streaks.
explain: Scientists use indicators too: litmus paper, made from lichens, turns red in acid and blue in alkali. Universal indicator mixes several dyes, so each pH gets its own colour, a whole rainbow from red to purple.
explain: Indicators hide in everyday life: lemon makes tea paler, a curry stain turns red when washed with soap, and pool keepers check the water every day with drops and a colour card.
fact: An indicator is a substance whose colour shows whether something is acidic or alkaline.
fact: Red cabbage juice is purple in neutral water, pink-red with acid, blue-green with a mild alkali like baking soda, and yellow with a very strong alkali.
fact: The colour substance in red cabbage changes shape at different pH, and each shape has a different colour.
fact: Blueberries have the same kind of colour substance, so they turn greenish in batter with too much baking soda; a little lemon juice brings the purple back.
fact: Litmus paper turns red in acid and blue in alkali; its dye comes from lichens and has been used for hundreds of years.
fact: Tea's colour substances change in acid: lemon makes tea paler, and baking soda makes it darker.
fact: Beetroot juice stays red in vinegar and turns more purple-brown in alkali; not every plant colour changes the same way.
fact: Beetroot can make your wee pink, which is harmless.
fact: Universal indicator is a mix of several indicators; each changes at a different point, so together they give a different colour for each pH.
fact: Scientists also use electronic pH meters.
fact: Yellow turmeric turns reddish-brown with an alkali, so a curry stain can turn red when washed with soap.
fact: Pool test kits compare the colour of a water sample with drops added to a colour card; water that is too acidic stings the eyes.
fact: Lemon-juice writing is invisible until a grown-up warms the paper, when the juice turns brown before the paper does; spies once used invisible inks like this.
exercise: What is an indicator? | A substance whose colour shows acid or alkali | A machine that measures how hot or cold things are | A substance that cancels acids | A kind of strong acid
exercise: What colour does red cabbage juice turn with vinegar? | Pink-red | Blue-green | Yellow | It stays purple
exercise: What colour does red cabbage juice turn with baking soda? | Blue-green | Pink-red | Black | Clear
exercise: What colour would lemon juice make the cabbage water? | Pink-red, because lemon is an acid | Blue-green, because lemon is an alkali | Yellow, the colour of lemons | It would stay purple
exercise: Why can one cabbage give so many colours? | Its colour substance changes shape at different pH | It contains a separate dye for every single colour | The light in the room keeps changing | The water is a different temperature each time
exercise: Why do blueberry pancakes sometimes have green streaks? | Too much baking soda changes the blueberry colour | The blueberries were picked before they were ripe | Mould grew in the batter overnight | The pan was made of copper
exercise: What colour does litmus turn in acid? | Red | Blue | Green | Yellow
exercise: Where does litmus dye come from? | Lichens | Red cabbage | Squid ink | Iron rust
exercise: What happens to tea when lemon is added? | It gets paler | It gets darker | It turns green | It turns bright pink
exercise: Does beetroot change colour the same way as red cabbage? | No, it stays red in acid | Yes, exactly the same | No, it never changes at all | Yes, but only when frozen
exercise: Why mix several indicators to make universal indicator? | Each changes at a different pH, covering the whole scale | One indicator alone is far too expensive to buy | Mixing them together makes all the colours brighter | It makes the indicator safe to drink
exercise: Why did the curry stain turn red in the wash? | Turmeric turns red with alkaline soap | The washing machine was too hot | Red socks were in the same wash | The curry had beetroot in it
exercise: Why do pool keepers test the water every day? | To check it is not too acidic or alkaline | To check how warm or cold the water is today | To count the swimmers in it | To see whether it needs refilling
exercise: Why does lemon-juice writing appear when warmed? | The juice turns brown before the paper | The heat melts a hidden layer of invisible wax | The paper turns clear around it | Lemon glows in the dark when hot
exercise: What else, besides dyes, can scientists use to measure pH? | An electronic pH meter | A thermometer | A magnifying glass | A weighing scale
exercise: Is pink wee after eating beetroot dangerous? | No, it is harmless | Yes, see a doctor at once | Yes, it means an infection | No, but it lasts for a year
test: Leo adds a mystery white powder dissolved in water to cabbage juice and it goes green. What does that tell him? | The powder is alkaline | The powder is acidic | The powder is neutral | The cabbage has gone off
test: "my blueberry muffins came out GREEN wtf". What probably happened? | Too much baking soda in the batter | The berries had gone mouldy in the fridge | The oven was too cold | Green food colouring got in
test: A spy cartoon shows lemon writing appearing by itself after an hour. What's the real way? | It shows only when warmed by a grown-up | It shows after an hour in the dark | It shows if you breathe on it | It shows when soaked in cold water
test: Zara drops red litmus into a cup of soapy water. What will she see? | It turns blue | It stays red | It turns yellow | It turns black
test: If you dropped universal indicator into lemon juice and then into bleach, what would you see? | Two very different colours | The same colour both times | No colour at all both times | The cups would start fizzing
test: Mum says every coloured fruit and veg changes colour exactly like red cabbage. What's true? | Not all: beetroot behaves differently | Yes, every plant colour is the same | Only green vegetables change | Only fruit, never vegetables
test: "the pool's making my eyes sting like mad". What could the test kit show? | The water may be too acidic | The water is too cold | There are too many swimmers | The water is pure and neutral
test: Gran adds lemon to her black tea and it goes paler. Grandpa says it's because lemon is yellow. What's true? | Acid changes the colours in the tea | Yellow lemon juice mixes with brown tea | The lemon cools the tea down | Lemon soaks up the tea leaves
test: Which would turn red cabbage juice pink? | Vinegar | Baking soda | Soap | Plain water
test: "my white shirt had curry on it and now the stain's gone RED after washing??" Why? | Turmeric turns red with alkaline soap | The washing machine added its own red dye | The curry was secretly tomato | Hot water burns stains red
test: If a lab had no indicators at all, how could a scientist still measure pH? | With an electronic pH meter | By tasting every liquid | By weighing the liquid | By looking at it under a lamp
exam: Identify the term designating a substance whose colour reveals whether a solution is acidic or alkaline. | An indicator | A neutraliser | A catalyst | A solvent
exam: It is given that red cabbage juice is added to a solution of baking soda. Determine the colour most likely to be observed. | Blue-green | Pink-red | Colourless | Deep black
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises universal indicator? | It is a mixture of indicators producing a distinct colour for each pH | It is a single dye derived exclusively from red cabbage | It changes colour only in the presence of strong acids | It is a device that measures pH by electricity
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning litmus. | It turns red in acid and blue in alkali, and its dye is obtained from lichens | It turns blue in acid and red in alkali, and its dye is obtained from cabbage | It remains unchanged in acid and turns green in alkali | It is a recent invention of the present century
